<BaseLayout title="Flowline Entanglement" description="Topological conjugates and the 'Crease' mechanism of measurement.">
  <article class="container" style="padding-top: 4rem; padding-bottom: 4rem; max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto;">
    <h1>Flowline Entanglement</h1>
    <p class="lead">Entangled particles are not two separate objects communicating across space. They are two intersection points of the exact same static flow line viewed on a single "page" of our timeline.</p>

    <figure style="margin: 2rem 0; text-align: center;">
      <div style="background: #f1f5f9; height: 300px; border-radius: 12px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; color: #64748b; border: 2px dashed #cbd5e1;">
      <img
        src="/images/09-flowlines.jpg"
        alt="Hydrodynamic Particle Map: Dual-dial topology showing intersection depth (mass) and tilt (charge)" 
        style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 12px; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);" 
        loading="lazy"
        />
      </div>
      <figcaption style="color: #64748b; font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: 0.5rem; font-style: italic;">Fig 3. A single TSC flow line intersects the timeline at two points: the Entry and the Exit.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Opposite Spins as Geometric Chirality</h2>
    <p>A flow line has an orientation. When our timeline slices through it, the Entry and Exit points are geometric mirror images—like a right hand pressed against a windowpane emerging looking like a left hand on the other side.</p>
    <p>The relationship $S_{exit} = -S_{entry}$ is a mechanical requirement, not a mysterious correlation.</p>

    <h2>The "Crease" Mechanism</h2>
    <p>Measurement forces a <strong>Temporal Crease</strong> ($\Gamma \to 1$). The timeline physically crumples around the flow line at that spot.</p>
    <p>Because the Entry and Exit are the same object, locking the geometry at one end instantly locks the other. There is no faster-than-light signal traversing space; there is only a failure to recognize that what appears as two objects in 3D space is one object viewed through the thickness of a 4D boundary.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>